bricx-common-passwords.txt — the Keycloak password denylist of the bricx realm
(P6-05e, ADR 0081). Read by Keycloak's passwordBlacklist policy from
/opt/keycloak/conf/password-blacklists/ (infrastructure/docker/keycloak/Dockerfile).

Source
  SecLists, Passwords/Common-Credentials/10k-most-common.txt
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/danielmiessler/SecLists/master/Passwords/Common-Credentials/10k-most-common.txt
  fetched 2026-11-09
  upstream sha256 68782d6a4a19a4768d5f15dd66bd534e7a33055cc755411e33f16d18c50fdcce
  (10,001 lines)

Why this list (owner ruling Q5, 2026-11-09)
  The NCSC "100k most used passwords" list (from Have I Been Pwned) was the
  first choice. Its redistribution in a public repository is not clearly
  permitted: the NCSC page that published it has been removed, NCSC's Open
  Government Licence excludes third-party material, and the HIBP Terms of
  Use (clause 2.1.4(d)(iii)) restrict distributing HIBP data. So this repo
  ships the SecLists 10k list under SecLists' MIT licence instead.
  Gap: only 10 of its entries are 12 characters or longer, so with the
  realm's length(12) rule this list adds little beyond the length rule
  (ADR 0081 records it; P14-01 revisits the list and the length).

Transform (reproducible)
  tr -d '\r' < 10k-most-common.txt | awk '{print tolower($0)}' | awk 'NF' | LC_ALL=C sort -u
  i.e. lowercase (Keycloak compares a lowercased password with lowercase
  entries), no blank line, LF line endings, byte-order sorted, unique.
  Result: 10,001 lines,
  sha256 a8af3b97296db20f7f3f788467d3e13d5bd7ddc1f74ddd4923dd52fd941796cb

Updating it
  Re-run the transform on the new upstream file, update both sha256 values
  and the line count here and in tooling/keycloak-realm/test/realm-policy.test.ts,
  rebuild the image (pnpm dev:up builds it), then run the realm tests.

